# Complexity and diff review

Before you commit or open a PR on **substantive code changes**, run these two checks. They catch implementation defects and complexity bloat that tests alone often miss.

## 1. abcop — complexity gate

```sh
abcop --full
```

Match CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`). Fix every offense before finishing:

| Rule | Limit | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Metrics/AbcSize` | ≤ 17 per function | Extract helpers; simplify control flow |
| `Metrics/ModuleAbcSize` | ≤ 120 per module | Split into a coherent sub-module |

Do **not** silence abcop with disable comments or raised thresholds. Refactor until clean.

Treat `UsedOnce` / `NeverUsed` warnings as inline opportunities — remove dead locals and fold one-shot bindings when it clarifies the code.

## 2. git diff — semantic review

```sh
git diff -w -W --histogram --no-prefix
```

Read the diff as a reviewer would:

- **`-w`** — ignore whitespace so you see logic, not formatting churn
- **`-W`** — show whole functions so edits stay coherent in context
- **`--histogram`** — emphasize the changed lines
- **`--no-prefix`** — drop `a/` / `b/` noise

Look for defects and unnecessary growth:

- Redundant branches, duplicated logic, or helpers used once
- Over-abstraction (new types or layers that do not pay for themselves)
- Missed simplifications — can the same behavior be shorter and clearer?
- Changes unrelated to the task (drive-by refactors, debug code, commented-out blocks)

Prefer the **smallest correct diff**. If abcop or the diff review exposes a problem, fix it in the same PR — do not leave complexity or slop for a follow-up.

## When to skip

- Docs-only, config-only, or fixture-only changes with no production logic
- The user explicitly asks to skip review (`/no-test` or equivalent)
